\title{The Ordered Field of Transseries:\\ Asymptotic Expansions Beyond Power Series}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-28}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a rigorous model of single-tower, real-power \emph{transseries} --- formal
series in the iterated exponential--logarithm scale
$\ldots, e^{e^x}, e^x, x, \log x, \log\log x, \ldots$ --- and establish their fundamental
algebraic and order-theoretic structure. Realizing transseries as Hahn series over the
lexicographically ordered group of transmonomials $\Lex(\Z \to_f \R)$ with real
coefficients, we prove that they form a \textbf{field}, and indeed a \textbf{non-Archimedean
ordered field}: the transmonomial $x$ is a positive infinitesimal, its reciprocal $1/x$ is
infinite, and the two multiply to $1$. We show that the real numbers embed as a linearly
ordered subfield, that exponential growth dominates every power ($x^a < e^x$ for all real
$a$), that the value group is unbounded with the canonical exponential tower cofinal, and
that the exponential substitution $x \mapsto e^x$ is a field automorphism whose inverse is
the logarithmic substitution. Our central analytic result is the \textbf{asymptotic
comparison theorem}: two transseries that agree to all orders are equal --- a transseries is
uniquely determined by its asymptotic expansion. We ground every formal order statement in
classical real analysis via little-$o$ comparison. We close with the program toward
real-closedness, for which the value-group divisibility and transmonomial root-extraction
ingredients are now in place.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The power series is the central tool of local analysis, but it is structurally incapable of
expressing the comparison that dominates global asymptotics: that $e^x$ outgrows every
polynomial. The exponent set of a power series is totally ordered with a successor structure
(the integers, or $\mathbb{Q}$), and no element of such a set dominates all of
$\{x^n : n \in \N\}$ at once. Transseries, introduced in the work of \'Ecalle,
Dahn--G\"oring, and developed extensively by van den Dries, Macintyre, Marker, van der
Hoeven, and Aschenbrenner--van den Dries--van der Hoeven, repair this defect by enlarging the
monomial scale to include iterated exponentials and logarithms.

This paper formalizes the foundational layer of the theory for \emph{single-tower,
real-power, grid-based} transseries and proves the structural theorems that distinguish them
from power series, organized around five pillars: the field structure (\S3), the order
structure (\S4), the multiplicative algebra (\S5), the asymptotic comparison theorem (\S6),
and the exponential symmetry (\S7). All results have been formally verified.

\section{Preliminaries: Hahn series}

Let $\Gamma$ be a linearly ordered abelian group and $R$ a ring. A \textbf{Hahn series} over
$\Gamma$ with coefficients in $R$ is a function $f : \Gamma \to R$ whose support
$\{\gamma : f(\gamma) \neq 0\}$ is \emph{well-ordered}. Hahn series form a ring under
pointwise addition and convolution multiplication
\[
(f \cdot g)(\gamma) = \sum_{\alpha + \beta = \gamma} f(\alpha)\, g(\beta),
\]
the well-ordering of supports guaranteeing finiteness of the sum. When $R$ is a field and
$\Gamma$ a linearly ordered group, the Hahn series form a \textbf{field}
$R(\!(\Gamma)\!)$ (Hahn--Mal'cev--Neumann); inverses are computed by the geometric expansion
of $(1-u)^{-1}$ for $u$ of strictly positive valuation.

The \textbf{valuation} of a nonzero Hahn series $f$ is the least element of its support,
written $\ord(f) \in \Gamma \cup \{\top\}$ with $\ord(0) = \top$; it is multiplicative,
$\ord(fg) = \ord(f) + \ord(g)$. The \textbf{leading coefficient} $\lc(f)$ is the coefficient
at $\ord(f)$. When $R$ is a linearly ordered domain, $R(\!(\Gamma)\!)$ is ordered by
$0 < f \iff 0 < \lc(f)$, making it an ordered field over a field $R$.

\section{The field of transseries}

\subsection{Transmonomials}

\begin{definition}[Transmonomial group]
The group of \textbf{transmonomials} is
$\TransMono := \Lex\,(\Z \to_f \R)$, the finitely supported functions $\Z \to \R$ under
pointwise addition with the \textbf{lexicographic order}: for $f \neq g$, the sign of $f-g$
is the sign of its value at the \emph{least} index where they differ. An element records a
finite tuple of real exponents indexed by \textbf{tower height} $h$ (height $1$ is $e^x$,
$0$ is $x$, $-1$ is $\log x$, $2$ is $e^{e^x}$). We encode height $h$ at index $-h$, so higher
towers occupy smaller indices and are lexicographically most significant.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
For $h \in \Z$, $a \in \R$, set $\mono(h,a) := \toLex(\delta_{-h}\!\cdot a) \in \TransMono$,
the single-support finsupp with value $a$ at index $-h$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Dominance laws]\label{lem:dom}
\textup{(\texttt{mono\_lt\_mono\_of\_height}, \texttt{mono\_lt\_mono\_same})}
If $h < h'$ and $0 < a'$, then $\mono(h,a) < \mono(h',a')$ for any $a$ \emph{(height
dominance)}; and if $a < a'$, then $\mono(h,a) < \mono(h,a')$ \emph{(same-height
comparison)}.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply the lexicographic criterion at index $-h'$ (resp. $-h$): both finsupps vanish at
strictly smaller indices, and the deciding index carries positive value $a'$ (resp.
$a'-a > 0$).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exponential dominance]\label{thm:expdom}
\textup{(\texttt{exp\_dominates\_pow})} For \emph{every} real exponent $a$,
$\mono(0,a) < \mono(1,1)$, i.e. $x^a < e^x$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Lemma~\ref{lem:dom} with $h=0<1=h'$, $a'=1>0$. A single fixed object $e^x$
dominates the entire family $\{x^a : a\in\R\}$, impossible in any power-series valuation whose
value group is $\Z$ or $\R$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The field}

\begin{definition}
The \textbf{field of transseries} is
$\TSeries := \R(\!(\TransMono)\!) = \mathrm{HahnSeries}(\TransMono, \R)$. The
\textbf{one-term transseries} $\trm(h,a)$ has coefficient $1$ on $\mono(h,a)$ and $0$
elsewhere. We abbreviate $x := \trm(0,1)$, $e^x := \trm(1,1)$, $\log x := \trm(-1,1)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Field]\label{thm:field}
\textup{(\texttt{C\_injective})} $\TSeries$ is a field, and the constant embedding
$C : \R \to \TSeries$ is an injective ring homomorphism; hence $\R \hookrightarrow \TSeries$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The Hahn--Mal'cev--Neumann instance applies as $\TransMono$ is a linearly ordered abelian
group and $\R$ a field; injectivity of $C$ is the constant-embedding injectivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Valuation]
\textup{(\texttt{orderTop\_term}, \texttt{orderTop\_mul})}
$\ord(\trm(h,a)) = \mono(h,a)$ and $\ord(x\cdot y) = \ord(x) + \ord(y)$.
\end{proposition}

\section{The non-Archimedean ordered field}

\begin{definition}
Equip $\TSeries$ with the leading-coefficient order to obtain
$\OTSeries := \Lex(\TSeries)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Ordered field]\label{thm:of}
\textup{(\texttt{orderedField})} $\OTSeries$ is simultaneously a field, a linear order, and a
strict ordered ring; i.e. a genuine ordered field. The positivity criterion is
$0 < f \iff 0 < \lc(f)$.
\end{theorem}

\paragraph{Orientation subtlety.} The lexicographic order decides at the smallest index, and
height $h$ is stored at index $-h$; the conventions compose so that the field order is the
germ order at $x \to 0^+$: $\mono(h,a) > 0 \iff a > 0$, independently of $h$. Thus $x$
(exponent $+1$) is infinitesimal and $1/x$ (exponent $-1$) is infinite.

\begin{theorem}[Positivity]\label{thm:pos}
\textup{(\texttt{term\_pos})} For all $h,a$, $\;0 < \trm(h,a)$ in $\OTSeries$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the positivity criterion, $\lc(\trm(h,a)) = 1 > 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$x$ is a positive infinitesimal]\label{thm:inf}
\textup{(\texttt{x\_pos}, \texttt{x\_infinitesimal})} $0 < x$, and $(n+1)\cdot x < 1$ for
every $n \in \N$; thus $x < \tfrac{1}{n+1}$ for all $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(n+1)x = \mathrm{single}(\mono(0,1), n{+}1)$. The difference $1 - (n+1)x$ has least support
element the constant monomial $0$ with coefficient $+1 > 0$, so by the lexicographic criterion
$(n+1)x < 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$1/x$ is infinite]\label{thm:infinite}
\textup{(\texttt{inv\_x\_infinite})} For every $n \in \N$, $\;n < \trm(0,-1) = 1/x$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $1/x \le n \le n+1$, multiplying by $x>0$ with $x(1/x)=1$ gives $1 \le (n+1)x$,
contradicting Theorem~\ref{thm:inf}; and $n \neq 1/x$ since their valuations differ
($\mono(0,-1)$ is a negative-index monomial, $\ord(n)$ the constant monomial or $\top$).
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reciprocal pair]\label{thm:recip}
\textup{(\texttt{x\_mul\_inv\_x})} $x \cdot (1/x) = \trm(0,1)\cdot\trm(0,-1) = 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By the law of exponents (Theorem~\ref{thm:exp}), $\trm(0,1)\trm(0,-1) = \trm(0,0) = 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Non-Archimedean]
$\OTSeries$ is a non-Archimedean ordered field: it contains a positive infinitesimal $x$ and
an infinite element $1/x$ that are mutually reciprocal. No such elements exist in $\R$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Ordered real embedding]\label{thm:Cembed}
\textup{(\texttt{C\_lt\_iff}, \texttt{C\_strictMono})} For real $a,b$,
$C(a) < C(b) \iff a < b$; with Theorem~\ref{thm:field}, $\R$ is a linearly ordered subfield.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$C(b)-C(a) = C(b-a)$ has leading coefficient $b-a$; apply the positivity criterion.
\end{proof}

\section{The multiplicative algebra and the value group}

\begin{theorem}[Law of exponents]\label{thm:exp}
\textup{(\texttt{term\_mul\_term\_same}, \texttt{term\_zero}, \texttt{term\_mul\_neg},
\texttt{term\_pow})} For fixed height $h$:
\[
\trm(h,a)\trm(h,b) = \trm(h,a{+}b), \quad \trm(h,0)=1, \quad \trm(h,a)\trm(h,-a)=1, \quad
\trm(h,a)^n = \trm(h, na).
\]
Hence every one-term transseries is a unit \textup{(\texttt{isUnit\_term})} and
$a \mapsto \trm(h,a)$ is a homomorphism $(\R,+) \to \TSeries^{\times}$
\textup{(\texttt{termHom})}.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Single-monomial multiplication reduces to additivity of $\delta_{-h}$:
$\delta_{-h}a + \delta_{-h}b = \delta_{-h}(a{+}b)$, lifted through $\toLex$; the power law is
induction on $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded value group]\label{thm:exists}
\textup{(\texttt{exists\_gt})} For every transmonomial $g$ there exists $g'$ with $g < g'$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $\ofLex(g)$ has least support index $i_0$, add a unit at $i_0-1$: the result first differs
from $g$ at the new least index $i_0-1$ with positive value. If $g=0$, any positive monomial
works.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Non-Archimedean dominance of valuations]\label{thm:powexp}
\textup{(\texttt{pow\_var\_lt\_exp}, \texttt{orderTop\_varX\_pow})} For every $n \in \N$,
$\ord(x^n) = \mono(0,n) < \mono(1,1) = \ord(e^x)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\ord(x^n) = \mono(0,n)$ by the power law; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:expdom} with $a=n$. The
tower-height coordinate (index $-1$) outranks the power-of-$x$ coordinate (index $0$).
\end{proof}

\section{The asymptotic comparison theorem}

\begin{definition}[Agreement to all orders]
$\Agree(a,b) :\Longleftrightarrow \forall g \in \TransMono,\ g < \ord(a-b)$ (compared in
$\TransMono \cup \{\top\}$): the difference is asymptotically smaller than every
transmonomial.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Asymptotic comparison theorem]\label{thm:cmp}
\textup{(\texttt{agreeToAllOrders\_iff\_eq})} $\Agree(a,b) \iff a = b$. A transseries is
uniquely determined by its asymptotic expansion.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) If $\ord(a-b) \neq \top$ it equals some $c$; instantiating at $g=c$ gives
$c<c$, absurd. So $\ord(a-b)=\top$, i.e. $a-b=0$. ($\Leftarrow$) If $a=b$ then
$\ord(a-b)=\ord(0)=\top$ strictly dominates every $g$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
\textup{(\texttt{agreeToAllOrders\_equivalence}, \texttt{not\_agree\_zero\_of\_ne\_zero})}
Agreement to all orders is an equivalence relation (it is equality), and any nonzero
transseries fails to agree to all orders with $0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Analytic grounding]\label{thm:litto}
\textup{(\texttt{isLittleO\_pow\_exp}, \texttt{isLittleO\_expPow\_expExp})} For every
$n \in \N$, as $x \to +\infty$: $x^n = o(e^x)$ and $(e^x)^n = o(e^{e^x})$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The first is the standard polynomial-vs-exponential estimate; the second composes it with
$e^x \to +\infty$. These ground $\mono(0,n) < \mono(1,1)$ and $\mono(1,n) < \mono(2,1)$.
\end{proof}

\section{The exponential substitution as a field automorphism}

\begin{definition}
Let $\sigma(i)=i-1$, $\tau(i)=i+1$. The \textbf{exp-substitution} $E$ and
\textbf{log-substitution} $L$ are the ring homomorphisms on $\TSeries$ induced by relabeling
transmonomials along $\sigma$, $\tau$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Action on terms]
\textup{(\texttt{expShift\_term}, \texttt{logShift\_term}, \texttt{expShift\_var})}
$E(\trm(h,a)) = \trm(h{+}1,a)$ and $L(\trm(h,a)) = \trm(h{-}1,a)$; so $E(x)=e^x$,
$E(e^x)=e^{e^x}$, $E(\log x)=x$, and $E(C(r))=C(r)$ \textup{(\texttt{expShift\_C})}.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Order preservation]
\textup{(\texttt{shift\_lt\_iff})} The height-shift is an order isomorphism of $\TransMono$;
exp-substitution preserves asymptotic dominance.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
A lexicographic comparison is decided at the least index of difference; a monotone bijection
of indices preserves least-index-of-difference, hence the order.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Field automorphism]\label{thm:auto}
\textup{(\texttt{expShiftEquiv}, \texttt{expShift\_logShift}, \texttt{logShift\_expShift})}
$E$ is a field automorphism $\TSeries \xrightarrow{\sim} \TSeries$ with inverse $L$:
$E\circ L = \mathrm{id} = L\circ E$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Injectivity from injectivity of the index translation; surjectivity (and two-sided inverse)
because $\sigma,\tau$ are mutually inverse bijections of $\Z$, so the round-trips reduce to
$\mathrm{id}$, lifted to coefficients via the domain-embedding coefficient calculus.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cofinality of the exponential tower]\label{thm:cofinal}
\textup{(\texttt{exists\_exp\_tower\_gt})} Every transmonomial $g$ is strictly dominated by
some iterated exponential: $\exists n \in \N,\ g < \mono(n,1)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $i_0$ the least support index of $\ofLex(g)$, choose $n = (1-i_0)^+$ so that $-n < i_0$;
then $\mono(n,1)$ has its positive deciding coefficient at $-n$, below every support index of
$g$.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm A (Transmonomial comparison).} Scan exponent maps from the smallest
index (highest tower); return the sign of the first nonzero exponent difference. Complexity
$O(k)$ in occupied heights $k$.

\paragraph{Algorithm B (Multiplication).} Form all pairwise products of transmonomials (add
exponent maps, multiply coefficients) and collect equal transmonomials. Complexity $O(mn)$.

\paragraph{Algorithm C (Reciprocal).} For $f = c\,\mu\,(1+\varepsilon)$ with infinitesimal
$\varepsilon$, $f^{-1} = c^{-1}\mu^{-1}\sum_{k\ge 0}(-\varepsilon)^k$, truncated at the desired
order; successive terms have strictly larger valuation.

\section{Applications}

Transseries provide unique asymptotic expansions for solutions of algebraic differential
equations at irregular singular points (via Theorem~\ref{thm:cmp}); the algebraic substrate
for resurgent/non-perturbative expansions in physics (via \S4); the dominance skeleton of
Hardy fields and o-minimal structures (via Lemma~\ref{lem:dom}, Theorem~\ref{thm:auto}); and a
cofinal yardstick (Theorem~\ref{thm:cofinal}) for super-polynomial complexity growth rates.

\section{Discussion and future work}

We have established that single-tower, real-power transseries form a non-Archimedean ordered
field containing $\R$, with an explicit infinitesimal/infinite reciprocal pair, an unbounded
value group made cofinal by the canonical exponential tower, an exp/log substitution symmetry,
and a uniqueness-of-expansion theorem. The orientation analysis fixes the field order as the
germ order at $x \to 0^+$.

The next target is \textbf{real-closedness}, which for a Hahn field factors into:
real-closedness of $\R$ (classical); divisibility of the value group (now in hand:
$\Lex(\Z\to_f\R)$ is divisible, unlike the Laurent value group $\Z$); and root extraction for
$1+\varepsilon$ with $\varepsilon$ infinitesimal (binomial series). Completing the binomial
layer would prove that every nonnegative transseries is a square and that the transseries
field is real closed.

